                        "expression": "CRL branch CriterionRef L138C15"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "input": [
                    {
                      "extension": [
                        {
                          "url": "http://hl7.org/fhir/uv/cpg/StructureDefinition/cpg-input-text",
                          "valueString": "Is the actual upper lid MRD 2.0 mm or less, with brows relaxed?"
                        },
                        {
                          "url": "http://hl7.org/fhir/uv/cpg/StructureDefinition/cpg-input-description",
                          "valueMarkdown": "Use the actual eyelid margin in the eye under review, not the overhanging skin margin. MRD is the distance from the corneal light reflex to that margin. This example uses the ordinary resting measurement."
                        }
                      ],
                      "type": "Observation",
                      "profile": [
                        "http://example.org/crl/StructureDefinition/l34194-bleph-example-ptosis-margin-requirement-met"
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "extension": [
                        {
                          "url": "http://hl7.org/fhir/uv/cpg/StructureDefinition/cpg-input-text",
                          "valueString": "Do the photographs document the qualifying lid position?"
                        },
                        {
                          "url": "http://hl7.org/fhir/uv/cpg/StructureDefinition/cpg-input-description",
                          "valueMarkdown": "Frontal photographs of both eyelids must support the actual-margin MRD for the eye under review, with sufficient quality and detail, patient name and date, and no distorting tilt. A lid obstructing the pupil is a clear-cut finding. Photographic measurement assumes an approximately 11 mm corneal diameter. If the corneal diameter deviates by more than 0.5 mm, document that deviation or include a millimeter ruler approximately in the corneal plane. An ordinary corneal diameter needs neither deviation documentation nor a ruler."
                        }
                      ],
                      "type": "Observation",
                      "profile": [
                        "http://example.org/crl/StructureDefinition/l34194-bleph-example-ptosis-photographs-documented"
                      ]
                    }
                  ],
